---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Kikimi: learn a language from videos you own">
  <h1 style="margin-top: 40px">Learn a language from videos you already have.</h1>
  <p>
    Kikimi listens to your videos on your Mac, finds the words you will meet, and helps you learn them with spaced
    repetition, using real sentences from the videos. Spanish and Japanese, with more to come.
  </p>
  <!-- The link goes in when there is a notarized release to point at. -->
  <a class="button" aria-disabled="true" href="#" tabindex="-1">Download for Mac (coming soon)</a>
  <p class="small">Free. Needs macOS 26 or later; Apple silicon recommended.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Add a video.</strong> Drop in a file you own. Nothing is downloaded from video sites.</li>
    <li><strong>It transcribes on your Mac.</strong> A speech model you download once does the work; nothing is uploaded.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose your words.</strong> See what you already know, start learning the rest, and look words up as you watch.</li>
    <li><strong>Practise.</strong> Review words at the right time, each with a sentence from your own videos.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Made for comprehensible input</h2>
  <p>
    Each video shows how much of it you already understand, so you can pick material that is mostly familiar with a
    little new. One button starts learning the commonest words that would make a video comfortable.
  </p>

  <h2>Private by design</h2>
  <p>
    No accounts, no ads, no analytics. Your videos, transcripts and progress stay on your Mac. The only time Kikimi
    goes online is to download a speech model when you ask. Read the <a href={`${base}/privacy/`}>privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
